  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure will remain over southern Quebec today into \nThursday night as a dry cold front crosses from the north and \nJosephine passes well offshore. Partly to mostly sunny skies \nwill prevail with highs into the lower 60s south of the mtns \nwith 50s across the north. The increasing pressure gradient \nforce from distant Subtropical Storm Josephine and high centered\nto our NW will allow for increasing winds throughout the day \nwith N gusts up to 25-35 mph likely from this afternoon through \nthe first half of Friday.\n\nJosephine will continue to move northeast, well out to sea. \nWith persistent northerly flow aloft, expect a chilly and \nblustery day on Friday with highs mainly in the mid 40s to mid \n50s. The gusty winds will make it feel even colder. \n\nAs winds diminish Friday night, a mostly clear sky should prove \noptimal for radiational cooling and very cold temperatures. The \nlatest NBM is advertising lows in the 20s across the entire forecast \narea and this may end up being on the warm side depending on the \npresence of high clouds.  \n\nHigh pressure then dominates the weather pattern through the weekend \nas an upper level ridge nudges into the region. This will lead to a \ngradual warmup through the weekend and into next week. Highs on \nSaturday should mainly be back in the 50s with some 60s returning \nSunday and Monday. A cold front will sweep through on Monday which \nwill lead to cooler temperatures into midweek. As of now, this cold \nfront looks to be dry but we could end up seeing some low \nprecipitation chances as we move closer in time.",
